Metal–organic  framework  and  carbon  hybrid  nanostructures: Fabrication strategies and electrocatalytic application for the water splitting and oxygen reduction reaction
Ziyun $\mathsf{s u}^{1}$ , Qiupin Huang1, Qian Guo1, S. Jafar Hoseini2, Fuqin Zheng1 $(\Join)$ , and Wei Chen1 (✉)
1 School of Chemistry and Pharmaceutical Sciences, Guangxi Normal University, Guilin 541004, China   
2 Rashidi Laboratory of Organometallic Chemistry & Material Chemistry, Department of Chemistry, College of Sciences, Shiraz University, Shiraz   
7194684795, Iran
## ABSTRACT
With  the  rapid  development  of  economy,  the  increasing  energy  crisis  and  environmental  pollution  urge  us  to  develop sustainable  and  clean  novel  energy  systems.  Among  them,  the  electrochemical  energy  conversion  technology  is considered as one of the ideal potential alternative energy systems, and the electrocatalysts play critical roles but are still challenging.  Metal-organic  frameworks  (MOFs),  thanks  to  their  regular  channels,  atomically  dispersed  active  centers, adjustable  chemical  and  pore  environments,  have  severed  as  promising  electrocatalysts  for  electrochemical  energy conversion. However, the relatively low conductivities and instabilities of MOFs limit their wide application in this field. In this case, fabricating hybrids of MOFs and carbon-based materials is an effective way to overcome above deficiencies. In addition, the synergistic effects between MOFs and carbons could optimize the electronic structures of active sites and promote  the  active  surface  areas,  and  thus  improve  the  electrocatalytic  performances  of  the  composites.  Herein,  we outline the current development of MOF/carbon composites, including the fabrication methods of MOFs hybridized with various dimensions of carbon-based materials and the electrocatalysis utilization for water splitting, including the hydrogen evolution  reaction  (HER),  oxygen  evolution  reaction  (OER),  and  the  oxygen  reduction  reaction  (ORR).  Finally,  the advantages and challenges of such catalysts are highlighted and future endeavors on the development of MOF/carbon composites for the HER, OER and ORR are discussed.
## KEYWORDS
energy conversion, metal-organic frameworks, carbon materials, hydrogen evolution reaction, oxygen evolution reaction, oxygen reduction reaction, water splitting, electrocatalysis
## 1    Introduction
With  the  rapid  development  of  modern  society,  the  renewal  of energy  storage  and  energy  conversion  technologies  are unavoidable  requirements  to  meet  the  increasing  demand  of human  life  [1, 2].  Electrochemical  oxidation  and  reduction reactions are the basis of a variety of energy conversion devices [3–5].  Among  them,  the  redox  reactions  between  hydrogen, oxygen and water have raised increasing concerns in recent years, for  their  critical  roles  in  many  energy  processes,  including metal–air batteries, water electrolysis, fuel cells, and so on [6–10]. In  particular,  the  increasing  amount  of  fluctuating  renewable energy in the grid renders such conversion reactions attractive for intermittent  chemical  energy  storage  [11, 12].  As  for  various energy conversion technologies, electrode materials are one of the most  important  components,  which  can  play  decisive  roles  in determining  the  electrochemical  performances  of  the  energy conversion devices [13–17]. To promote the practical applications of  these  energy  devices,  efficient  electrocatalysts  are  required  to lower  the  reaction  energy  barrier  and  improve  the  energy efficiency [18–22]. However, at present, the optimal electrocatalysts  are  precious  metals,  like  Pt,  Ru,  which  are expensive  and  few  reserved  [23, 24].  Therefore,  looking  for  new functional  materials  with  high  activity  and  good  stability  has always  been  the  top  priority  of  future  electrochemical  energy storage and conversion technologies.
Metal-organic  frameworks  (MOFs),  also  known  as  porous coordination polymers (PCPs), is a class of framework materials with  periodic  spatial  network  structures  formed  by  the  selfassembly of organic ligands and metal ions or metal ion clusters [25–28]. By applying the crystal engineering, the optical, electrical, magnetic,  and  biological  properties  can  be  introduced  into  the coordination frameworks. Combining the advantages of inorganic and organic components even can produce dual-function or multifunction composite molecular materials [29–33]. The design and preparation  of  MOFs  has  been  a  hot  research  area  in  recent decades  [34–37].  Nowadays,  many  kinds  of  MOFs  have  been widely utilized in gas storage and separation [38], chemical testing [39, 40], chemical sensing [41, 42], photocatalysis [43], supercapacitor  [44, 45]  and  electrocatalysis  [46–50],  and  made unbelievable progress, largely promoting the development of both such composites and their applications. Notably, MOFs are one of the  ideal  candidates  for  electrocatalysis  because  of  their  high specific  surface  area,  adjustable  pore  size  and  metal  nodes, selectable functionalized organic ligands, and cavities capable for accommodating  guest  molecules  [51, 52].  Nevertheless,  what cannot  be  ignored  is  that  MOFs  still  have  some  disadvantages, which  restrict  their  applications  to  a  great  extent  [53].  For instance,  MOFs  exhibit  inferior  performance  at  electrically conduction  and  have  a  poor  stability,  preventing  them  from demonstrating  their  best  performances  in  the  field  of electrochemistry [54].
In order to circumvent the above-mentioned challenges, three strategies  have  been  developed:  (i)  Single  precious  metal ions/atoms (such as Pt and Au) or multiple non-precious metals are injected into MOFs [55–58] ; (ii) MOFs are transformed into functional materials by calcination process [59, 60]; (iii) Building MOF-based  composites  [61–63].  Compared  to  the  former  two strategies, fabrication of MOF-based composites by using MOFs and  other  appropriate  materials  is  easier  to  handle,  which  can combine the advantages of both parent materials, and hence give rise to synergistic effects and new possibilities that the individual parts  never  get  access  to  [64–67].  Until  now,  MOF-based composites, such as MOF-metal nanoparticles (NPs) [68], MOForganic  polymers  [69]  and  MOF-carbon  [70],  have  been successfully constructed. Among them, MOF-carbon composites (namely  composites  of  metal–organic  frameworks  and  carbonbased materials) seem interesting, as MOFs represent a kind of the state-of-the-art  materials  while  carbon-based  materials  are identified  as  somewhat  classical  material  [66, 71–73].  The MOF/carbon  composites  can  integrate  the  advantages  and mitigate the shortcomings of the individual components perfectly, enabling the composites to possess improved stability, enhanced electrical conductivity, high selectivity, and template effects, thus realizing the application of MOF-based materials in electrochemistry  [74–76].  Thus,  it  is  worth  carrying  out  further studies focusing on their development and usage [77, 78].
Although  some  previous  reports  have  provided  excellent overviews  on  some  types  of  composites  of  MOFs  and  other materials,  such  as  two-dimensional  materials/MOF  composites [79, 80],  graphene-based  materials/MOF  composites  [81, 82], NP/MOF composites [83, 84], and so on [85, 86], there have been few reviews that focus on the synthesis methods of carbon/MOF composites  and  their  applications  in  energy  conversion. Considering the promise of MOF/carbon-based materials, it is of great  significance  in  mastering  the  design  principle  and summarizing  the  synthetic  strategy  of  various  MOF-carbon composites, which will bring new inspirations for the development of MOFs composites for energy conversion. Therefore, this review aims to describe, summarize, and analyze metal-organic skeleton and  carbon-based  material  composites  and  their  synthesis  and applications  in  energy  conversion,  including  the  hydrogen evolution reaction (HER), oxygen evolution reaction (OER), and the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR), as schematically shown in Fig. 1.
## 2    Preparation methods of MOF/carbon composites
A composite consists of at least two components which contribute to the whole system together but retain their own identity at the same time [87]. Generally, the dominating component is regarded as  the  matrix  material  and  the  others  as  the  functional  species. Nevertheless, here it is difficult to classify the synthesis methods based on this principle, as the synthesis methods for MOF-based and  carbon-based  composites  share  some  similarities  and sometimes  cannot  be  distinguished  clearly  [74].  Therefore,  we divide  these  composites  according  to  the  spatial  dimensions  of carbon  materials,  including  zero-dimensional  (0D,  carbon  dots), one-dimensional (1D, carbon nanotubes, carbon nanofibers), twodimensional (2D, graphene), and three-dimensional (3D) carbons (including  porous  carbon  material,  carbon  sphere,  etc.),  and outline their synthesis strategies.
## 2.1    Zero-dimensional carbon dots and MOF composites
Carbon dots (CDs), including carbon quantum dots (CQDs) and graphene quantum dots (GQDs), are carbon nanomaterials with sizes less than $10\ \mathrm{nm}.$ , which can be synthesized by top-down and bottom-up methods [88]. In this type of composites, MOFs with high  porosity  and  specific  surface  area  provide  as  exciting platforms,  creating  an  ideal  environment  for  loading  CDs  and preventing them from aggregation. Meanwhile, CDs enhance the physicochemical  properties  of  MOFs.  As  a  result,  the amalgamation of CDs and MOFs results in good dispersion and great  stability  [36, 89].  In  these  composites,  the  CDs  can  settle either  on  the  external  surfaces  or  in  the  channels  of  MOFs.  At present, different synthesis methods have been reported to couple CDs with MOF matrixes. Here, the synthesized methods of CDMOF  composites  were  summarized  with “ship-in-a-bottle”, “bottle-around-the-ship” and  others  [90].  The “ship-in-a-bottle” and “bottle-around-the-ship” are  normally  used  to  encapsulate CDs into the cavities of the MOF supports.
## 2.1.1    Ship-in-a-bottle method
The “ship-in-a-bottle” method needs to firstly infiltrate or deposit
CDs precursors into the preformed MOF matrix, and then attain the  target  composite  by  further  treatment.  Until  now,  some strategies  have  been  developed  for  the  introduction  of  CDs precursors  into  MOFs,  which  can  be  divided  into  solution impregnation and chemical vapor impregnation. Specifically, the solution  impregnation  method  is  considered  to  be  the  simplest method and it is also widely used for MOFs with loading other guest materials [91, 92]. For example, in a recent report from He et al., the CDs precursor of glucose solution was firstly adsorbed directly into an ideal template of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) via solution impregnation  and  the  precursor  was  then in  situ converted  to uniformly  dispersed  CDs  via  a  facile  low  temperature carbonization treatment (Fig. 2(a)). The in situ formed CDs were confirmed to homogeneously disperse within the cages of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - MIL-125(Ti) [93]. In another work, Lin et al. utilized the pores of MIL-53(Fe)  as  encapsulated  nanoreactors  to  access  and  enrich glucose molecules by solution impregnation for the confinement of  various  amount  of  CQDs  in  MIL-53(Fe)  (Fig. 2(b)).  The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image showed that a lot of tiny CQDs with size of ca. $1.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ were formed in the pores of MOFs [43].
Compared  with  the  common  solution  impregnation  method, the double solvent method (DSM) is able to avoid the deposition of  guest  materials  on  the  surface  of  MOFs  [66].  For  example, Meng  et  al.  used  the  one-step  DSM  to  co-fix  the  precursors  of CDs and CdS quantum dots into the cages of MIL-101 as follows. The  MIL-101  was  firstly  dispersed  homogeneously  in  a  large amount  of  n-hexane  (hydrophobicity),  and  subsequently  the precursor aqueous solution (hydrophilicity) with volume slightly less than the pore volume of MIL-101 was added dropwise into the above hydrophobic solution, which could be diffusion into the hydrophilic pore of MIL-101 by capillary force (Fig. 2(d)). Then, the  synthesized  CD/CdS@MIL-101(50)  composite  was  formed through  a  heating  treatment.  In  this  strategy,  two  functional groups were encapsulated uniformly into the pores of MOFs with size around $3\mathrm{nm}$ [94].
Besides the above solution impregnation method, Albolkany et al. proposed a chemical vapor permeation technology to confine graphite-carbon nitride quantum dots (g-CNQDs) into HKUST-1 (Fig. $2(\mathrm{c})_{\r}^{\r}$ ).  The  selected  precursor  of  cyanamide  (CA)  has  low melting point $(47\mathrm{~\textdegreeC})$ and high vapor pressure, and it could be catalytically  condensed  to  form $\mathbf{g}$ -CNQDs.  CA  was  introduced into  the  pores  of  the  activated  HKUST-1  at  low  temperature. During this process, HKUST-1 served as a bi-functional material. On one hand, HKUST-1 could effectively activate CA under mild conditions.  On  the  other  hand,  it  can  control  the  condensation and assembly to yield uniform $\mathrm{\Deltag-CNQDs}$ in confined pore spaces at low temperatures. Under these mild conditions, HKUST-1 was structurally intact with the size of $\mathbf{g}$ -CNQDs strictly limited to 2.22 $\pm0.68\mathrm{nm}$ [95].
Since  the  impregnation  process  requires  heating  or  long-time immersion to synthesize CDs, this method is not suitable for some MOFs to confine CDs, because the structures of these MOFs may be destroyed or partially broke under heating treatment or in hot and humid environment.
## 2.1.2    Bottle-around-the-ship method
The “bottle-around-the-ship” is  a  method  assembling  the precursors  of  MOFs  around  the  pre-synthesized  CDs,  and  thus forming the CD-MOF composites. In this synthesis strategy, the pre-synthesized  CDs  are  firstly  dispersed  in  a  solvent,  which contains  stabilizer  such  as  surfactant,  to  prevent  CDs  from agglomeration. Then, MOF precursors were added into the above solution,  initiating  MOF  assembly  around  the  CDs  [96].  Since CDs are pre-synthesized before assembling the frame, the size and shape can be adjusted according to the application [97]. Based on this  method,  Hou  et  al.  synthesized  MOF@CCQDs/NiF composites by depositing chiral CQDs (CCQDs) on the surface of Cu-based  MOF  in  a  three-electrode  system.  In  the  study,  the treated  Ni  foil  (NiF)  was  used  as  the  working  electrode,  the platinum  plate  and  calomel  electrode  serve  as  the  counter  and reference electrode, respectively, and the mixed solution of CCQD suspension  and  the  Cu-MOF  precursor  was  used  as  the electrolyte.  The  characterization  results  showed  that  the $\mathrm{MOF@CCQDs/NiF}$ composites  maintain  the  framework  of  the used MOF [98]. Rehman et al. incorporated pre-synthesized CDs into  nickel-based  mixed  linker  MOF  by  solvothermal  method using $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine  (bpy)  and  1,3,5-benzene-tricarboxylic  acid $\mathrm{(H_{3}B T C)}$ as organic linkers. However, it was found that the CDs were decorated in the cavities, rather than on the surface of the formed  MOFs  [99].  Hong  et  al.  constructed  the  unique heterogeneous  Co-ZIF/CDs/CC  (ZIF $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ zeolitic  Imidazolate Framework)  composite  by  embedding  CDs  within  Co-ZIF nanosheet  arrays  on  carbon  cloth  (CC) via an in  situ coupling strategy  (Fig. 3(a)).  During  this  process, ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions  were  firstly adsorbed onto the surface of CC, and then co-precipitated with 2- MIM (2-methylimidazole) to form the Co-ZIF nanosheet array on CC,  followed  by  the in  situ confinement  of  CDs,  due  to  their quantum size, high water solubility and tendency to attach to ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ . Nevertheless, the CDs were fixed on the surface of $\mathrm{Co}$ -ZIF, and some were even separated from Co-ZIF [100].
To overcome the above-mentioned drawbacks, many strategies were then proposed. The normal used method is modifying CDs with functional groups, like −COOH, $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}.$ $-\mathrm{OH}$ , to strengthen the  coupling  interaction  between  MOFs  and  CDs.  For  instance, Mou et al. integrated functional CQDs (such as −COOH, $\mathrm{-NH}_{2},$ −OH) with NiFe-MOF via a solvothermal method. The functional groups made CQDs combine well with the matrix and thus the CQDs were homogeneously embedded into the MOF channels to obtain  the  CQDs@NiFe-MOF.  Even  though  CQDs@NiFe-MOF was calcinated at $160^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the CDs were still uniformly dispersed in the  MOFs  [101].  Dong  et  al.  developed  a  type  of  nitrogen  and sulfur  co-doped  carbon  dots  (N,  S-CDs)  to  coordinate  with  the MOF  precursors  (Fig. 3(c)).  As  a  result,  the  Eu-MOFs  were nucleated and grown on the surface of CQDs, thus leading to the encapsulation  of  N,  S-CDs  in  the  Eu-MOFs  [102].  In  another work, Song et al. utilized carboxylate CQDs (CQDs-COOH) as a structure regulator to induce the in situ fabrication of 2D NiFeMOF  nanosheets  (NSs) via a “bottom-up” synthesis  strategy  at room-temperature  (Fig. 3(b)).  This  synthesis  strategy  effectively avoided  the  low-yield  exfoliation  processes  and  additional complex. Due to the enlarged layer spacing of NiFe-MOF and the electron-withdrawing  of –COOH  groups,  the  CQDs-COOH induced  the  formation  of  NiFe-MOF  NSs  and  made  CQDs successfully couple to the MOF substrates [103].
Usually,  due  to  the  larger  size  of  CDs  than  the  pore  size  of MOFs,  the  CDs  are  unavoidably  deposited  on  the  surface  of MOFs.  For  instance,  Zhang  et  al.  mixed  pre-synthesized  acidic functionalized  CDs  with  the  precursor  solution  of  MOF-802  to obtain CDs@MOF-802 composites by solvothermal reaction (Fig. 3(d)).  The  TEM  images  indicated  that  the  mean  size  of  CDs  is much  larger  than  the  aperture  of  MOF-82  (2  nm  vs.  5.6  Å). Therefore,  the  CDs  actually  settle  on  the  external  surface  rather than  in  the  cavities  of  the  MOF-82 via the  coordination  of  the metal node with the −COOH group [104].
## 2.1.3    Other synthesis methods
Beside  above-mentioned  strategies,  there  are  still  other  methods for  the  fabrication  of ${\mathrm{CDs/MOF}}$ composites,  and  they  can  be divided into two types. One is the commonly used strategy that directly compounds the pre-synthesized samples of both the CDs and  the  MOFs  to  form  CDs/MOF  hybrids.  Due  to  the  smaller micropores  of  the  MOFs,  the  CDs  are  mainly  attached  on  the outer  surface  of  MOFs.  For  example,  Wang  et  al.  prepared  the $\mathrm{CQDs}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125  composite  by  a  solvent-deposition  strategy (Fig. 3(f)) [105]. In the synthesis, the pre-synthesized samples of both  CQDs  and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125  were  dispersed  into  the  distilled water, resulting in the deposition of CQDs onto the surface $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - MIL-125.  Similarly,  Feng  et  al.  constructed  CQDs@UiO-66- $\mathrm{(COOH)}_{2}$ composite film by combining the pre-prepared samples of both the CQDs and MOF (Fig. 3(e)). Interestingly, the authors fabricated UiO-66 using $^{1^{\prime},2^{\prime},4^{\prime},5^{\prime}}$ -benzenetetracarboxylic $\mathrm{(H_{4}B T E C)}$ acid as organic linker instead of terephthalic acid. Thus, two uncoordinated carboxylic groups were presented on the pore surface, which can not only generate negative charges to facilitate the  positive  deposition  of  film,  but  also  prevent  CQDs  from aggregation [106].
Another method is to mix the precursor solutions or solids of both the CDs and the MOFs together, inducing the simultaneous formation of MOFs and CDs via a one-step process. For example, Yu’s  group in  situ prepared $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe)/CDs  composites by  a  solvothermal  method.  In  the  synthesis,  the  precursor solutions  of  both  CDs  and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe)  were  mixed together,  and  then  heated  at  high  temperature via one-pot solvothermal method. As a result, the CDs were in situ generated on the surface of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) [107]. This one-step synthetic method  is  considered  to  be  simple,  environment-friendly  and efficient.  However,  the  different  synthesis  conditions  for  MOFs and CDs make this method applicable to specific reaction systems rather than all reactions [90].
## 2.2    One-dimensional carbon and MOF composites
The  typical  1D  carbon  materials  include  carbon  nanotubes (CNTs),  carbon  nanofibers  (CFs,  including  carbon  cloth  (CC)), graphene nanoribbons (GNRs), and so on. Their common feature is the ultrahigh aspect ratio and flexibility, which gives them a high specific  surface  area  to  enable  them  to  combine  with  other materials.  For  the  1D  carbon-MOF  composites,  due  to  the periodic  porous  structure  of  MOFs,  the  1D  carbons  may  be confined into the channels of the MOFs. Meanwhile, MOFs can also nucleate and grow on the surface of the 1D carbon. That is, in such  composites,  MOFs  can  act  as  both  matrixes  and  guest materials. Since most 1D carbon structures are synthesized under harsh conditions, such as superhigh temperature, the 1D carbonMOF composites are normally formed via compounding the preobtained  1D  carbons  with  either  MOF  precursors  or  presynthesized MOFs. Hence, four main strategies have been used for preparing  such  type  of  composites:  one-step  method,  stepwise method, ex situ approaches and other synthesis strategies [74].
## 2.2.1    One-step method
One-step  method  is  a  timesaving  and  resource-conserving strategy. In this method, carbons were directly mixed with welldissolved MOF precursors and then the composites can be formed under  the  same  procedure  for  the  formation  of  pure  MOFs (hydrothermal or solvothermal process) [74]. This method avoids a long separation and purification process and it can be used to fabricate almost all types of carbon-MOF hybrids. Based on this method, in a recent report, CNTs were added into the precursor solution  of  copper  phthalocyanine-based  (PcCu)  2D  conjugated
MOF to construct the PcCu- ${\cal{O}}_{8}$ -Co/CNT composite, using squareplanar cobalt bis(dihydroxy) complexes $(\mathrm{Co-O_{4}})$ as linkages [108].
As MOFs are available to either assemble on the surface of 1D carbons  or  confine  1D  carbons  into  their  frameworks,  various morphologies of 1D carbon-MOF composites can be attained. In a recent  work  from  Yaqoob  et  al.,  bimetallic  FeNi  MOF  particles were uniformly dispersed on the surface of CNTs substrate via a single step solvothermal method. The uniform dispersion of the pre-obtained  MOFs  on  the  CNTs  surface  was  ascribed  to  the hydrophobic  nature  and  huge  surface  area  of  CNTs,  which prevented the CNTs from being immobilized into the hydrophilic channels  of  MOFs  and  also  avoided  the  aggregation  of  MOF particles [109]. In another study, Yu et al. constructed a flower-like CoNi  MOFs-MWCNTs  (MWCNTs $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ multi-walled  CNTs) composite NSs via hybridizing MWCNTs with CoNi MOF by a solvothermal  method.  In  the  synthesis  process,  MWCNTs  were used  as  a  kind  of  structural  regulator.  During  the  synthesis process, the flexible MWCNTs were interwoven with these MOF NSs and caused great steric hindrance to prevent the preformed MOF  particles  form  agglomeration  to  spheres.  In  addition, experiments indicated that the MWCNTs could effectively control the composite size. With the content of MWCNTs increasing, the size of the composite also increases [110].
Besides,  surface  of  1D  carbons  can  also  be  covered  by  MOF arrays, especially using CFs as substrates. To coat MOFs densely and tightly on these 1D carbons, it’s necessary to modify the outer frame  of  1D  carbon  with  functional  groups,  which  could  be obtained from the strong acid treatment [111–113]. For instance, Qiang et al. fabricated Ni-based MOFs NSs on CNTs by a facile one-step solvothermal approach (Fig. 4(a)). The CNTs were preformed into films (CNFs) and treated in dilute nitric acid to enrich the carboxyl groups on their surfaces. As a result, the MOFs were grown vertically, densely and tightly on the surface of CNFs[111] Jin  et  al.  constructed  NiFe-MOF in  situ grown  on  CC $\left(\mathrm{[Ni_{2.87}F e_{0.13}(H H T P)_{2}]}_{n}/\mathrm{CC},\right.$ HHTP $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ hexahydroxytriphenylene) by using a traditional one-step hydrothermal method (Fig. 4(b)). Before  the  MOFs  growth,  the  used  CC  was  treated  with concentrated  nitric  acid  for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ to  ensure  abundant  oxygencontaining functional groups on its surface. Therefore, the formed regular NiFe-MOF nanorods were densely and tightly bound onto the  CC  skeleton  [112].  Similarly,  Geng  et  al.  also  fabricated  2D conductive MOF nanorod arrays on CC (CuCo-CAT/CC, CAT $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ catecholate) via a  facile  solvothermal  technique  [114].  The outcomes  showed  that  the  CuCo-CAT  arrays  were  densely  and perpendicularly grown with diameter and length around 0.3 and $3.0\upmu\mathrm{m};$ , respectively.
To  confine  CNTs  into  the  channels  of  MOFs,  CNTs  should generally be modified with heteroatoms or groups. For example, in a recent report, acid-modified MWCNTs were combined with MOFs  by  a  one-pot  solvothermal  process.  The  experimental results  indicated  that  the  acid-treated  MWCNTs  induced  the heteronucleation  and  the  growth  of  MOF  crystals  on  MWCNT carboxylic  groups,  resulting  in  the  complete  incorporation  of MWCNTs inside the bulky MOF-5 without breaking the crystal structure of MOF-5 [115].
However, due to the long CNTs and the small size of MOFs, it’s usually difficult for a single CNT to be immobilized into a MOF particle. For example, in a recent work, Bi-TCPP MOFs (bismuth metal  porphyrin-based  MOFs,  TCPP $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ tetra  (4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin)  were  wrapped  around  the  external  surface  of  the acidified CNTs with a unique sandwich structure (Fig. 4(d)). The SEM  scanning  electron  microscopy  (SEM)  and  TEM  images showed  that  there  were  still  small  segments  of  CNTs  out  of encapsulation  with  MOF  [116].  Jung  et  al.  fabricated  ZIF$67@\mathrm{CNT}$ composite  with  a  structure  like ‘sushi  kebab’ through consecutively bridging individual ZIF-67 particles onto the CNT substrate (Fig. 4(c)). The synthesis process is as follow: $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ cations were  firstly  connected  to  the  functional  groups  of  CNTs via electrostatic  attraction,  and  then  induced  the  heterogeneous nucleation  and  growth  of  ZIF-67  onto  CNT  surfaces.  The formation of this unique ‘sushi kebab’ structure may be related to the nucleation and growth rates, which was not discussed in the paper [117].
## 2.2.2    Stepwise method
When the crystallization of MOFs with 1D carbon is difficult by a one-step  synthesis,  stepwise  synthesis  may  offer  an  efficient alternative  way  [74].  In  this  method,  1D  carbons  are  firstly activated  by  functional  materials,  such  as  MOF  seeds,  metal oxides, and then the growth of MOFs on their surface is achieved. According to the functional materials, the stepwise method can be divided into three categories: non-covalent method, seeded growth method and MOF precursors transformation method.
In the case of non-covalent modification, molecules are coated on 1D carbon or stack on the surface of CNTs through numerous $\pi{-}\pi$ superposition  effects  [118].  For  example,  Zeng  et  al. functionalized  the  CNTs  by  anchoring  cobalt  phthalocyanine $(\mathrm{CoPc})$ on their surface (CoCNT) through tough π-π interactions, which  effectively  prevented  the  aggregation  of  CoPc.  Then  the MOF  precursors  of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ were  mixed  with  the  CoCNT and  solvothermal  reaction  was  conducted  to  grow  UiO- $66\mathrm{-NO}_{2}$ on CoCNT (UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNO_{2}@C o C N T}$ ). In this composite, there are two kinds of non-covalent interactions, one is between $\mathrm{CoPc}$ and CNTs and another is between $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ and CoCNT, leading to the formation of ultrafine UiO-66- $\mathbf{\cdotNO}_{2}$ NPs [120]. Differently,
Li et al. activated the cotton fabric with carboxyl polymer chain brushes via aromatic radical polymerization (MCF), which could provide  numerous  reactive  carboxymethyl  groups  for  the attachment  of  MOF  precursors  (Fig. 4(e)).  Then,  dense  CuBTC crystals with micro−nano size and regular octahedron were in situ grown layer-by-layer on the MCF. On the contrary, only sparse and  irregular  CuBTC  crystals  were  formed  on  the  pristine  CF surface [119]. This superior strategy is also called as layer-by-layer assembly (or liquid phase epitaxy), which can adjust the coating thickness of MOFs by controlling the growth periods [74, 121].
Seeded growth approach is used for the formation of 1D carbonMOF hybrids by firstly synthesizing and depositing seeds of MOF crystals  on  the  carbon  surface  and  then  inducing  the  further growth of MOFs. This method was proved to be available for the fabrication  of  MOF–carbon  membranes.  During  the  synthesis procedure,  the  nucleation  and  attachment  of  seeds  on  carbon surface are significantly important and can be realized via various strategies,  like  heating,  dip  coating  and  rubbing.  For  instance, Dumée et al. constructed a defect-free ZIF-8 membrane on porous CNT bucky-paper (BP) through a seeded growth method. Firstly, the  seeds  of  ZIF-8  crystals  were  homogeneously  anchored  onto the CNT surface at room temperature by directly placing the CNT BP in the precursor solution of MOFs. Then, the modified CNT BP was transferred and heated at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to induce ZIF-8 crystals to  be  packed  into  a  continuous  and  dense  network  [122]. Differently,  Zhang  and  coworkers  used  a  new  seeded  growth process to fabricate a low-defect and continuous ZIF-8 membrane on  macroporous  carbon  tube  (Fig. 5(a)).  The  seeds  were constructed by a dip-coated and rubbed (DCRM) strategy, which caused the composite of graphite and ZIF-8 NPs coating onto the carbon surface to obtain a stable seed layer. During the formation of seed layer, graphite played an important role, which not only smoothed  the  rough  surface  of  the  carbon  substrate  but  also anchored  and  stabilized  the  ZIF-8  seeds.  Subsequently,  a  dense ZIF-8  layer  was  formed  on  the  carbon via a  secondary solvothermal growth [123].
Besides, MOFs can also be synthesized through the conversion of precursor materials, like metal hydroxide, oxide and metal NPs. An advantage of this method is that the morphology of MOFs can be facilely controlled by controlling the morphology of precursor materials, as shown below. Cheng et al. prepared NiMn-MOF NSs on  the  surface  of  multi-channel  carbon  fiber  (MCCF/NiMnMOFs)  by  a  two-step  strategy  (Fig. $5(\mathrm{b})$ )  [124].  In  this  work, MCCF  was  firstly  covered  with  NiMn-LDH $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{(LDH~=~}}$ layered double hydroxide) NSs through a facile hydrothermal method and then a ligand exchange reaction was used to in situ convert NiMnLDH NSs to NiMn-MOF NSs. In this synthesis process, there are both  etching $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ ,  which  caused  local  dissolution  of  NiMn-LDH, and  chelation  of  organic  ligands,  which  promoted  rapid nucleation of MOFs on the MCCF surface. Eventually, the NiMnLDH  NSs  were  completely  transformed  into  NiMn-MOF  NSs, and  thus  forming  the  final  composite  of  MCCF/NiMn-MOFs. Similarly, we also in situ fabricated 1D sword-like FeNi-MOFs on CC surface by a semi-sacrificial template of FeNi-LDH (Fig. 5(c)) [125]. The as-prepared composite has specific 2D/1D combined structure with ultrathin 2D nanosheets ( $1.34\mathrm{nm}$ in thickness) and long 1D MOF nanorods $(1.3~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ in length). There are also two main  processes  in  the  formation  of  this  composite:  dissolution reaction (etching FeNi-LDH) and nucleation reaction (heterogeneous  nucleation  and  growth  of  MOF  on  CC). Therefore,  with  the  reaction  time  or  concentrations  of  organic linkers  increasing,  the  FeNi-LDH  became  thinner  and  thinner, while MOFs became thicker and longer.
Zha et al. used metallic Co NS arrays as MOF precursors for in situ conversion to MOFs on CC. The authors firstly deposited $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ NS  arrays  on  CC via an  electrochemical  deposition  route.  The metallic Co was then oxidized to ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ion by the $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ formed by hydrolysis of organic ligand, and thus ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions were combined with the organic ligand for the conversion to MOFs on the surface of $\mathrm{Co/CC}$ [126]. Due to the stronger oxidability of $\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}$ than $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ ion, the composite can be also obtained with the presence of $\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}$ ion. Therefore, the as-formed composite has a hierarchical hybrid structure, with outer MOFs nanorod arrays, middle Co NS arrays and inner carbon fibers. Dai et al. used nanosized $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ precursors and  MWCNTs  to  prepare  the  ZIF-8/MWCNTs  hybrid.  The synthesis  process  was  as  follows.  The  ZnO  NPs  were  firstly adsorbed  onto  the  surface  of  MWCNTs,  and  then  the  organic ligands 2-methylimidazole reacted with the adsorbed ZnO NPs to form  ZIF-8  crystals.  Thus,  compared  with  traditional  one-step method, more uniform and better-dispersed ZIF-8 crystals were combined tightly on MWCNTs [127].
## 2.2.3    Ex situ synthesis strategies
The  above-mentioned  methods  are  also  considered  as in  situ synthesis strategies, with which MOFs form with the presence of carbons.  These  methods  are  commonly  used  to  attain  the composites of MOF-carbon. However, in some cases, the undue amount  of  carbons  may  interfere  excessively  the  preparation  of MOFs, even disturb the carbon-MOF structures, resulting in poor properties.  Hence,  sometimes ex situ methods  are  adopted  to achieve 1D carbon-MOF hybrids, such as self-assembly and direct mixing.
Rani et al. synthesized the Sn-MOF@CNT nanocomposite by directly  mixing  the  assembling  Sn-MOF  with  CNTs via a sonochemical  strategy.  Due  to  the  huge  size  of  Sn-MOFs,  the CNTs were incorporated inside the Sn-MOF and resulted in the formation  of  carbon  layer  on  the  MOF  surface  [42].  Similarly, Wang  et  al.  constructed  the  composite  film  of  2D  CuTCPP/MWCNT  by  directly  mixing  the  ultrathin  2D  MOF  NSs with  MWCNT  through  a  ultrasonic  method  (Fig. 5(d)).  Due  to the  hydrophobic  effect  of  2D  Cu-TCPP  NSs,  MWCNTs  were firmly connected with these 2D MOF NSs and evenly dispersed in the film [129].
## 2.2.4    Other synthesis strategies
Most  of  1D  carbons  are  obtained  at  superhigh  temperatures. However, there are still a few 1D carbons which can be produced at relatively low temperatures, e.g., the GNRs polymerized from perylene. This makes it possible to post-form 1D carbon materials in/on  prefabricated  MOFs.  In  the  synthesis  process,  MOFs  can serve  as  templates  to  induce  directional  synthesis  of  1D  carbon materials.  Based  on  this,  Kitao  et  al.  inserted  atomically  precise GNRs into MOF channels by a post-polymerization method (Fig. 5(e)). The precursor of perylene vapor was firstly adsorbed into the  channels  of  MOF  under  reduced  pressure,  and  then  the composite  was  afforded  by  a  dehydrogenative  condensation reaction in a sealed tube at $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The outcome indicated that the formed  GNRs  have  up  to  30  monomer  units.  In  the  synthesis process, the nanochannels of MOFs play a significant role, which ensure  the  monomer  perylene  aligned  along  the  polymerization axis and thus lead to the propagation of GNRs in one dimension [130]. However, this method requires MOFs to be stable at high temperatures. Therefore, up to now, there are few studies in this field.
## 2.3    Two-dimensional carbon and MOF composites
Graphene  is  a  representative  2D  carbon  nanomaterial,  which comprises single or multiple $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ hybridized carbon atoms joined by  covalent  bonds  to  form  a  honeycomb  lattice  structure.  Its emergence  recently  opened  an  exciting  new  field  in  the  science and  technology  of  2D  nanomaterials  [131].  Notably,  graphenebased  materials  normally  have  ultrathin  thickness  (ideally  a monolayer)  and  large  lateral  size,  which  render  them  ideal substrates to hybridize other active materials. Compositing MOFs with graphene can effectively bypass the shortcomings and make best  use  of  the  advantages  of  each  component.  Especially, graphene  with  pyridine  or  carboxylate  groups  can  provide  the attachment sites and conduct the growth of MOFs on graphene [132].
Up  to  now,  various  graphene/MOF  composites  have  been obtained by different synthetic strategies, in which some syntheses depend on specific processes, while others use conventional onestep and stepwise methods. The solvothermal process is proved to be the most popular strategy for the synthesis of graphene/MOF hybrid  for  its  simplicity.  Meanwhile,  other  complicated approaches have also been exploited to synthesize composites with properties suitable for specific applications. Thus, the main known strategies are outlined in the following section [82].
## 2.3.1    Solvothermal method
Solvothermal  method  is  the  most  widely  used  method  for  the preparation  of  MOF/graphene  composites  because  of  its simplicity,  size-  and  morphology-controllability  and  moderate reaction  condition.  In  this  method,  the  MOF/graphene  hybrids are  formed  by  placing  the  precursors  in  appropriate  solvents (water,  organic  solvent  or  their  mixture),  followed  by  heating treatment through an in situ or ex situ synthesis process.
## 2.3.1.1    In situ synthesis method
For in  situ fabricating  MOF/2D  carbon  composites,  graphene oxide  (GO)  is  commonly  used.  The  surface  of  GO  is  generally covered  with  abundant  functional  groups,  such  as  -COOH  and -OH,  which  is  helpful  for  the in  situ growth  of  uniform  MOF. Metal sources firstly combine with the surface functional groups, and then MOFs would nucleate and grow on GO surface after the addition  of  organic  ligands.  This  type  of in  situ growth  can obviously enhance the binding force between graphene and MOFs [82, 133, 134].
For  example,  Hu  et  al.  fabricated  insulated  2D  MOF  on  raw graphene  surface via an  epitaxial  growth  strategy  by  a hydrothermal  method  (Fig. 6(a)).  During  the  synthesis  process, graphene  template  played  an  important  role  to  conduct  the nucleation  and  growth  of  2D  MOFs.  Precursor  molecules  first accumulated  on  the  graphene  surface via van  der  Waals interactions, and then 2D MOFs seamlessly grew and were highly oriented with large lateral extension. The result showed that the thickness and aspect ratio of this epitaxially grown 2D MOF are about 12 times higher than those of bulk MOF without graphene template. As a result, highly orientated and integrated MOF/GO composite  was  successfully  developed  [135].  Zheng  et  al. constructed bimetallic CoNi-MOF NSs on the surface of reduced graphene  oxide  (rGO)  by  one-step  solvothermal  method.  The CoNi-MOF NSs were in situ bound to rGO through the surface functional  groups  of  rGO  under  the  surfactant-assisted modulation  of  PVP  (polyvinyl  pyrrolidone).  In  this  synthesis process, rGO serves as pillar connector, which induced MOFs to contact with the surface functional groups. Meanwhile, PVP was used as surfactant that selectively adhered to the MOF nanocrystal surfaces  and  caused  the  anisotropic  growth,  resulting  in  the formation of CoNi-MOF NSs. Therefore, the obtained MOF NSs were homogeneously encapsulated by rGO [136].
To  further  strengthen  the  interaction  between  MOF  and graphene,  lower  lattice  mismatch  or  inserting  a  binder  between MOF and graphene is an effective way. For instance, Huang et al. synthesized  a  series  of  CoTCCP/graphene  hybrids  with  O/Ndoped graphene substrates obtained by three different processes: electrochemically  exfoliation  (EG),  ultrasound  (NG)  and  highpower ultrasound (NG’). In this synthesis process, the graphene supports were firstly combined with TCPP rather than metal ions, and  subsequently  the  adhered  organic  linkers  were  coordinated with  metal  nodes  to  form  MOFs  on  graphene  surface.  The structures  of  graphene  substrates,  such  as  size  and  nitrogen functionality,  were  critical  to  get  alignment  of  MOF.  The experiment results showed that the interfacial alignment between NG’ support  and  CoTCCP  effectively  lowered  the  symmetry mismatch and ultrahigh lattice mismatch, thus maximizing their contacts [137]. Hai et al. fabricated 2D ultrathin Ni-MOF NSs on graphene under the assistance of PBA (phenylenebisboronic acid) (HMOF $@$ GE-PBA).  Firstly,  PBA  was  successfully  bonded  to graphene  with  C-O-B  covalent  bond,  and  the  MOF  precursors were then combined with PBA and grown to NSs (Fig. 6(b)). Due to  the  high  orbital  delocalization  of  PBA,  the  covalent  linkages between  MOF  and  graphene  were  formed,  which  induced  the formation  of  ultrathin  MOF  NSs  with  uniform  thickness  of around $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The ultrathin flexible feature of MOF NSs made them  wrinkle  on  the  graphene  surface.  Nevertheless,  without adding PBA primer, MOF particles with shuttle-shape were selfnucleated and grown, independently from graphene sheets. Thus, the  assistant  PBA  primer  drove  the  MOFs  to  tightly  anchor  on graphene [138].
## 2.3.1.2    Ex situ synthesis
The ex situ synthesis of graphene/MOF composites is similar to the  fabrication  of  1D  carbon/MOF  hybrids,  i.e.,  directly  mixing the  pre-prepared  MOFs  with  graphene  or  its  derivatives  [74]. Graphene  in  the  reaction  mixture  does  not  interfere  with  the synthesis  of  MOF  in  comparison  with  the in  situ synthesis.  For instance, Jayaramulu et al. used a similar direct mixing strategy to prepare 2D $\mathrm{Ni(C_{4}O_{4})}$ $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}/$ graphene NSs (Fig. $6(\mathrm{c});$ ). In order to obtain  2D $\mathrm{{Ni}(C_{4}O_{4}){\cdot}(H_{2}O)_{2}/}$ graphene  NSs,  2D $\mathrm{{Ni}(C_{4}O_{4}){\cdot}(H_{2}O)_{2}}$ was first prepared by using $\mathrm{\Ni(OH)}_{2}$ as metal source, squaric acid $\mathrm{(H_{2}C_{4}O_{4})}$ as  organic  linkers  and  PVP  (polyvinylpyrrolidone)  as the structure-directing agent under solvothermal conditions. The metal  source $\mathrm{\Ni(OH)}_{2}$ effectively  eliminated  the  influence  of chloride and nitride anions and the template of PVP can induce MOFs to form nanosheets. Then the stripped graphene was added to  the  2D  MOF  ethanol  solution,  resulting  in  the  2D $\mathrm{Ni(C_{4}O_{4})}$ $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}/$ graphene NSs composite. In the formed composite, MOF dispersed over the graphene surface by electrostatic attraction, and therefore adhesion maybe exists between the MOF and graphene [139].
## 2.3.2    Sonochemistry methods
Power  ultrasound  has  been  employed  in  various  fields,  like chemical  and  processing  industries.  This  technique,  also  termed sonochemistry,  has  gained  attention  in  the  late  1970s  and  was defined  as  the  application  of  sound  waves  to  chemistry.  This technique  can  generate  high  temperature  and  pressure  during cavitation of bubbles, improving like reaction yield, kinetics and surface phenomena in liquid media, especially declining the time and  energy  consumption  [140].  During  the  MOFs  production, ultrasonic  wave  distribute  energy  evenly  throughout  the  liquid reaction medium, resulting in a higher crystallization rate of MOF than that of conventional heating.
For  exemple,  Dastbaz  et  al.  prepared $\mathrm{Cu-BDC-NH_{2}@G O}$ 1 $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{\mathrm{~H_{2}B D C=}}$ benzene dicarboxylic acid) hybrid by using ultrasonic method under low frequency and high-power ultrasound. Firstly, multilayer GO was exfoliated into homogeneously dispersed GO NSs via the insertion of high-energy jets from sonication between the layers. Then the mixture of MOF precursor with GO NSs was used  to  form $\mathrm{Cu-BDC-NH_{2}@G O}$ by  optimizing  the  GO concentration,  sonication  power  and  time.  With  the  increase  of GO content, the production yield was firstly improved and then declined since GO NSs impossibly received sufficient exfoliating energy to form dense GO. Thus, increasing the sonication power is  beneficial  to  exfoliate  multilayer  GO  flakes  into  NSs  in  the maximum  amount  and  then  improve  the  yield  of  composite. However,  as  the  sonication  power  increases,  the  reaction temperature  would  also  increase  until  reaching  boiling  point, while  high  temperature  would  result  in  metal  oxidation  and production  yield  decrease.  The  effect  of  ultrasonic  time  on production yield was similar to that of GO concentration. Excess time could increase the metal oxidation and thus open the binding between  metal  node  and  organic  linker.  This  method  largely reduces the conventional heating time of at least $12\mathrm{{h}}$ to $150~\mathrm{min}$ and the resulting MOF crystals are uniform, smaller or lamellar [141].
In another work, Ehrnst et al. reported an acoustic microfluidic strategy,  with  which  homogeneous ${\mathrm{q}}{-}2{\mathrm{D}}{-}{\mathrm{MOF}}/{\mathrm{rGO}}$ planar heterostructures were rapidly synthesized in milliseconds without any  additives  (Fig. 6(d)).  The  synthesis  process  of  this  hybrid involves  six  distinct  steps.  First,  MOF  precursor  was  delivered onto  the  acoustomicrofluidic  device.  Second,  a  thin  acoustic wetting  film  formed  under  the  surface  reflected  bulk  wave (SRBW). Meanwhile, GO sheets were arranged in flat layers and sword-like  MOF  was  crystallized  and  coordinated  between  the arranged  GO  layers.  Thirdly,  the  thin  film  was  nebulized  into aerosol  droplets  with  micron-size  and  was  loaded  on  MOF/GO hybrid.  Fourthly,  the  aerosols  and  hence  MOF/GO  composite were collected, and were then concentrated by centrifugation and washing. Fifth, this hybrid was transformed into a film via vacuum process.  Ultimately,  the  final  product  of  MOF/rGO  film  was attained by the selective reduction of MOF/GO film via UV/L-AA 1 $\mathbf{U}\mathbf{V}=$ ultraviolet, $\mathbf{\sigma}_{\mathrm{L}^{-}}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{A}=\mathbf{\sigma}_{\mathrm{L}}$ -ascorbic acid) approach. In particular, the  method  also  promotes  the  intercalation  plane  growth  and uniform in  situ crystallization  of  2D  MOF  structures  and  the arrangement of GO sheets [142].
## 2.3.3    Electrochemical synthesis
Electrochemical  synthesis  is  a  facile,  green,  fast  and  highly controllable  strategy  for  the  preparation  of  carbon-MOF composites, in which MOFs were directly electrodeposited on the conductive  support  and  ensured  tough  mechanical  connection and  effective  electronic  interaction  between  each  other.  Besides, during the electrochemical synthesis process, surfactants or other additives  are  generally  not  required.  However,  surfactants  or additives are commonly used in wet-chemical synthesis processes and  they  are  difficult  to  remove,  leading  to  contamination  of products.  There  are  also  some  shortcomings  in  this  synthesis method, such as complicated preparation mechanism, difficulty in controlling film thickness related to electron transfer kinetics.
Xu et al. in situ prepared a $\mathfrak{a}$ -CD-rGO/Ni-MOF/TM $\alpha{\mathrm{-CD}}=\alpha\cdot$ - cyclodextrin;  TM $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ Ti  mesh)  hybrid via a  two-step electrochemical  method.  In  this  work,  Ni-MOF  was  firstly electrodeposited  on  the  TM  surface  by  applying  a  constant negative  potential  to  the  working  electrode.  Then  cyclic voltammetry  (CV)  was  employed  to  convert  GO  to  rGO  and simultaneouslyα- $\mathrm{{CD/rGO}}$ was  electrodeposited  on  the  NiMOF/TM  surface.  This  synthesis  strategy  was  confirmed  to  be quick, facilely manipulated, environmentally friendly, efficient and controllable. The experimental outcomes displayed that the $\alpha$ -CD could effectively protect rGO NSs from aggregation and the $\alpha$ -CDrGO/Ni-MOF/TM  composite  was  successfully  obtained  with many rGO NSs-covered MOF NPs dispersed on the TM [143].
In a recent report from Lyu et al., NiFe-BTC//G composite was synthesized by a two-electrode electrochemical strategy (Fig. 7(a)). The synthesis process could be divided into three steps as follows. The first step is dilating a commercial graphite foil to graphene multilayers in sulfuric acid. The second step is inserting organic linkers  into  the  expanded  graphite/graphene  layers via an electrochemical strategy using the unique organic salt solution as electrolyte. The final step is placing the as-treated graphite foil in metal  salt  solution  to  attain  NiFe-BTC//G  composite.  The outcomes indicated that ultrasmall MOFs NPs were intercalated into 2D graphene multilayers [144].
## 2.3.4    Mechanical synthesis
Mechanical method is a facile and ex situ synthesis strategy, with which  the  reactants  are  physically  mixed  and  then  ground  in  a mortar  or  ball  mill  to  form  the  carbon-MOF  composites.  For example,  Tung  et  al.  prepared  three  different  graphene-MOF hybrids  by  first  placing  different  ratios  of  the  pristine  graphene (pG)  and  MOFs  (1:1  and  1:2)  in  a  mortar  to  pestle  and  the following sonication treatment [145]. The SEM and TEM images showed that small MOF particles were randomly distributed and fixed  on  the  graphene  surface.  The  MOF  particles  were  also inserted  between  the  graphene  sheets  to  form  a  sandwich-like structure, thus expanding the space of graphene platelets. Notably, the  crystal  structures  of  both  pG  and  MOFs  were  unchanged under pestle and ultrasonic sonication.
Compared with pestle in a mortar, ball milling is more complex and normally used with stronger mechanical energy. For example, Zhuang et al. prepared a composite of N-G/MOF using N-doped graphene and ZIF-8 as precursors by a nanoscale high energy wet ball  milling  (NHEW)  strategy  (Fig. 7(b)).  The  morphology  and chemical  structure  of  N-G/MOF  hybrid  were  confirmed  to  be closely  related  to  the  grinding  speed.  With  the  grinding  speed increasing  from  200  to $650~\mathrm{rpm}$ ,  the  particle  size,  porosity  and BTC surface area of N-G/MOF hybrid decreased, while the pore size increased from micropore to macropore. The most favorable outcome was attained at grinding rate of $350~\mathrm{rpm}$ . Notably, when the  grinding  speed  reached $650~\mathrm{{\rpm}}$ ,  the  MOF  would  be completely decomposed into amorphous carbon. The physicochemical properties of N-G/MOF indicated that the strong mechanical  energy  of  ball  milling  may  cause  chemical decomposition/restructuring  of  the  components  in  N-G/MOF [146].
Despite  its  simplicity,  this  mechanical  method  is  often disregarded  due  to  the  untunable  morphology  and  worse performance than that of graphene-MOF composites obtained by other ways.
## 2.3.5    Other synthesis methods
Beside  above-mentioned  strategies,  there  are  still  other  methods for graphene/MOF composites fabrication. For example, Sikdar et al.  proposed  a  diffusion-driven  synthesis  approach  to  directly modulate  the  growth  of  mono-metal/polymetallic  MOFs  on  a porous graphene (PG) framework (Fig. 7(c)). They employed the oil-in-water  interfacial  reaction  to  control  the  morphology  of nanomaterials  with  the  utilization  of  a  bi-phasic  system  of ‘graphene  hydrogel–organic  solvent’.  The  biphasic  system  was formed  as  follows.  The  graphene  hydrogel  was  firstly  saturated with  metal  ions,  and  then  was  mixed  within  an  organic  phase which  contains  organic  linkers.  During  the  synthesis  process, organic  linkers  first  diffused  from  the  organic  phase  to  the hydrogel-confined  aqueous  phase,  and  then  coordinated  with metal ions into MOFs. The ligand supply rate was depended on two  successive  phases:  the  hydrogel–organic  interface  and  the intra-hydrogel phase. This bidiffusion-driven method provides an ideal  condition  for  self-assembling  desired  MOF  structure.  As  a result, various heterostructures of MOF-graphene hydrogel (MOFPG hybrids) have been successfully synthesized. On the contrary, without  the  graphene  hydrogel  or  only  using  of  sole  aqueous phase, MOFs tend to grow more randomly [147].
Bian  et  al.  developed  an  interfacial  growth  strategy  based  on Pickering  emulsion  approach  to in  situ prepare $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}/\mathrm{GO}$ hybrids  (Fig. 7(d))  [148].  Pickering  emulsion,  which  is  a heterogeneous system consisting of at least two insoluble liquids, has been extensively applied in different fields. Different from the surfactant-stabilized  emulsion,  the  Pickering  emulsion  was stabilized via nano/micro colloidal particles. Its stability normally depends  on  the  wettability  of  the  particles  and  medium hydrophilic particles are more favorable to gather at the water/oil interface and stabilize the emulsion [149]. The amphiphilic GO with  oxygen-containing  functional  groups  (such  as $-\mathrm{O}-$ ‒COOH,  and  ‒OH)  has  been  considered  as  one  of  the promising Pickering emulsion stabilizers. The stability of the formed Pickering emulsion may be greatly affected by the size of  GO  NSs,  as  large  GO  NSs $(>5\ \upmu\mathrm{m})$ may  be  more hydrophobic  and  then  would  float  on  the  surface  of  water while  small  GO  NSs $(<1~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ may  be  less  hydrophobic  and then sink into the subphase. In the synthesis of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}/\mathrm{GO}$ hybrid, the metal source was dissolved in water phase and organic linkers was dispersed in n-octanol phase, and the two phases were put together to form water/oil emulsion stabilized by proper sized GO  NSs.  Then  the  organic  linkers  diffused  to  the  GO  surface under  the  action  of  a  concentration  gradient,  while  metal  ions were  anchored  on  the  GO  surface  by  coordination  and electrostatic  interaction.  Finally, $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}/\mathrm{GO}$ hybrid  was formed by the interfacial nucleation of MOFs on the GO surface, which stably floated at the water/oil interface [148].
Liu  et  al.  prepared  a  MOF/GO  composite  membrane via the intercalation of BTC-MOFs into the GO interlayer by means of vacuum filtration. The vacuum filtration technology is an ex situ synthesis  method,  which  is  facile  and  extensively  used  in  the fabrication of thin films. When placing a dispersion in the filter flask,  the  dispersion  will  be  deposited  onto  the  filter  membrane surface  continuously  to  form  a  film  under  the  action  of  the pressure  difference  in  internal  and  external  air  and  gravity. Adopting this method, a BTC−MOF-intercalated GO membrane was successfully fabricated [150].
## 2.4    Three-dimensional carbon and MOF composites
Generally  speaking,  3D  carbon  materials  refer  to  3D  porous carbon  materials,  also  known  as  bulk  materials,  including  bulk carbon  matrix,  porous  carbon  spheres,  etc.  [151, 152].  The combining of MOF with 3D carbon materials is mainly realized with the in situ synthesis method. Typically, 3D carbon materials are mixed evenly with the MOF precursor solution, and then the composite  material  is  synthesized  by  hydrothermal  method  or solvothermal process. For example, Kim et al. reported a method to  insert  the  Cu-MOF  into  the  pores  of  a  3D  porous  carbon material  (Fig. 8(a))  [153].  Firstly,  the  MOF  precursors  were introduced to fill the pore volume of these carbons via vacuum drying  process,  and  then  MOF  structure  was in  situ grown  in porous  carbon  channels  through  the  solvothermal  method. Moreover,  the  MOF  diameter  could  be  controlled  by  the  pore sizes of the selected 3D carbon matrixes, which even reached to 24 nm.
Beside the above vacuum method, the MOF precursor solution is also able to be encapsulated into the pores of the porous carbon material by using the double solvents method (DSM) [154]. For instance,  Shen  et  al.  used  a  two-step  DSM  to  introduce  MOF precursor solutions into multichambered carbon spheres (MCCS) to  form  a  series  of  MOF $@.$ MCCS  composites  (Fig. 8(b))  [155]. During  the  synthesis  process,  the  MCCS  could  serve  as  both interface stabilizers to form aqueous droplets of MOF precursors and  dispersants  to  distribute  them  evenly  in  the  solvent. Subsequently,  the  metal  ions  were  uniformly  dispersed  in  the MCCS cavities and drove organic ligands directional coordination with  them.  Therefore,  ultrafine  MOF  NPs  with  particle  size  as small  as $13\ \mathrm{nm}$ could  be  obtained  within  the  pores  of  MCCS, which  could  be  precisely  loaded via controlling  the  calcination extent and multichamber structure of MCCS.
Xiong and coworkers fabricated a yolk-shell structure of ZIF$67@\mathrm{{HMCS}}$ with  using  HMCS  (hollow  mesoporous  carbon sphere) as shell and ZIF-67 as core (Fig. 8(c)). The authors firstly synthesized a uniform HMCS with loose surface, a shell thickness of about $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ and cavity diameter up to $246~\mathrm{nm}$ . In addition, dopamine used as carbon precursor made the formed HMCS with abundant N heteroatoms doping $(6.4\%)$ , which provide rich sites to  anchor  metal  ions  and  induce  the  growth  of  MOFs  in  the HMCS cavity. Hence, the ZIF-67 particles with size around 100 nm were grown close to the internal wall of HMCS [70].
In  a  recent  work  from  Xie  et  al.,  3D  graphene  (3D  Gr)  was firstly  prepared  by  freeze-drying  the  rGO  hydrogel  and  then hybridized  with  Ni-MOF  using  the  ultrasonic-assisted  strategy. The  3D  Gr  is  fluffy  with  obvious  wrinkled  morphology  and porous structure. During the formation of the composite, the 3D Gr played a crucial role. Compared with the traditional rGO, the unique  spatial  stereoscopic  porous  structure  of  3D  Gr  provides abundant specific surface to be coated with the Ni-MOF particles. Therefore, the small Ni-MOF particles were dispersed uniformly on the surface of the 3D Gr. Meanwhile, the 3D Gr also effectively prevents the aggregation of the MOF NPs and avoids the blocking of active centers [156].
## 3    Application  of  MOF/carbon  composites  in electrocatalytic reactions
Due  to  the  unique  and  outstanding  properties  of  these  two intriguing materials, the carbon-MOF hybrids are expected to be applied in varieties of areas including and beyond that of pristine MOFs.  In  fact,  because  of  the  synergistic  effects  of  these  two components,  integration  of  the  superior  properties  and  new functionalities  make  carbon–MOF  hybrids  present  better performances  than  pristine  MOFs.  As  electrocatalysts,  the  key factors affecting the performances of carbon-MOF composites are the  conductivity,  stability,  surface,  textural  and  morphological nature. The conductivity and stability of carbon-MOF composites rely  on  the  combination  of  these  two  components,  as  stronger binding  and  more  contact  area  may  be  more  favorable. Meanwhile,  the  surface,  textural  and  morphological  nature  of composites have great influence on the number of effective active sites  and  the  mass  transfer  process,  as  larger  surface  area  and hierarchical  porous  structure  are  advantageous  for  the  catalytic processes.  Their  synergistic  effects  between  MOF  and  carbonbased material promote the number and intrinsic activity of active sites.
The improvement of electrocatalytic performances of carbonMOF  composites  is  closely  related  to  the  spatial  dimension  of carbon  materials.  The  conductive  0D  CDs  are  mainly  used  to circumvent  the  intrinsic  electrical  insulation  of  MOFs  and  thus promote the efficiency of electron conduction and electrocatalysis of the composites [157]. However, the unique structure and great specific surface area of 1D carbon materials can better address the drawbacks  of  MOFs  than  0D  CDs.  The  strong  interactions between MOFs and functionalized 1D carbons greatly accelerate the charge diffusion rate in the composites [64]. In particular, the network  structure  formed  by  1D  carbons,  such  as  CF,  CC,  can conduct  MOFs  to  form  array  structure,  which  is  beneficial  to expose abundant active sites and enhance the mass transfer. While graphene-based materials, who present carboxyl groups, pyridine groups  and  aromatic $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ structural  domains,  can  not  only participate in bonding interactions with MOFs, but also serve as structural nodes and even electrocatalytic active component. Based on this, graphene-based materials can promote the coordination bonds and induce MOFs to grow into a more beneficial structure [65].  3D  carbon-MOF  composites  are  relatively  rare  in electrocatalysis so far, but they are worthy of further study because of their special 3D structure.
Herein,  the  following  sections  focus  on  the  electrochemical performances of MOF/carbon composite for the HER, OER, ORR and bifunctional catalysis (overall water splitting, ORR and OER), and the results are listed in Table 1.
## 3.1    HER electrocatalysis
The  electrocatalytic  HER  is  a  cathodic  half-reaction  in  water splitting. Previous reports indicated that during the HER process, the  critical  factors  in  determining  reaction  efficiency  are  the chemisorption  and  desorption  of  intermediates  on  the  catalyst surface. The catalysts with strong binding with H atoms but with weak binding with the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ product are favorable for the HER. The detailed mechanisms of HER are generally different under various media. Under acidic condition, H atom intermediate is formed by coupling the proton from the solution with the electron from the electrode.  Then  two  neighboring  H  atoms  combine  together  or one H atom combines with another proton to form $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ with the following  releasing  from  catalyst  surface.  While  in  neutral  or alkaline electrolytes, because of lacking protons, H atoms should be formed from dissociating the absorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecule, which causes  the  property  degradation  of  catalysts  under  alkaline  or neutral media [160, 161].
At present, Pt is still considered as the optimal electrocatalyst for  the  HER  in  wide-range $\mathrm{\boldmath~\pH~}.$ To  improve  the  catalytic performances of other potential non-precious metal or metal-free electrocatalysts,  like  carbon-based  materials,  MOFs,  researchers have made great efforts. Due to the unique properties of MOFs, the  free  energy  of  hydrogen  adsorption  can  be  reduced  by modifying the electrode with MOF-based catalysts, providing an opportunity for this type of catalysts to outperform the benchmark catalyst of $20\%$ Pt/C. In the carbon-MOF composite, the catalytic active sites for HER mostly come from the metal nodes of MOFs and other active guest materials, the carbons mainly play a role in controlling the growth of MOFs to expose more active sites and improving  the  conductivity  and  stability  of  the  electrocatalysts. Though there are only few MOF-carbon electrocatalysts for HER so far, their great potential still warrants further study.
## 3.2    OER electrocatalysis
The OER, occurring at the anode, is a very important reaction in many energy conversion devices, such as metal–air batteries and water  electrolysis  cells.  The  OER  can  also  occur  under  different media, and the OER mechanism is complicated since the solution pH has effect on the catalytic activity and $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ and $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ can serve as active species in different electrolytes. Due to the above reasons, the  mechanism  of  OER  is  currently  still  under  debate  in  these different electrolytes. The commonly accepted theory is that the critical  factor  for  OER  is  the  binding  energy  between  the electrocatalyst and the oxygen-containing intermediates. Meanwhile,  during  the  OER  process,  most  metal  active  centers could be transformed to the corresponding oxides or hydroxides [3, 160, 162].
The OER is a 4-electron reaction, and it suffers from inherently slow  dynamics  and  high  overpotentials,  thus  requiring electrocatalysts  with  high  activities  to  overcome  the  sluggish kinetics [163]. However, the currently best OER catalysts of $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ are  precious  and  instable.  Transition  metals  and  their compounds have been found to be potential substitutes of these noble  metals,  and  MOFs  are  suitable  precursors  due  to  their controllable  structures  and  compositions  [164].  To  improve  the conductivity  and  activity  of  MOFs,  combining  carbon  materials with  MOF  is  proved  to  be  an  effective  strategy.  In  the  carbonMOF  composites,  the  metal  nodes  in  MOFs  are  normally  the active sites, while the carbons generally act as robust substrates to not only improve the conductivity and stability of MOFs but also induce  the  nucleation  and  growth  of  MOFs  with  favorable morphology.  Thus,  the  synergistic  effect  between  MOF  and carbon  is  conducive  to  obtain  electrocatalysts  with  outstanding OER properties.
0D carbons with various functional groups on their surface are proved  to  facilitate  their  hybrid  and  doping  with  different materials. When doped CDs into MOFs, they can greatly enhance the  efficiency  of  electron  transfer  and  thus  improve  the electrocatalytic  activity  of  composite  for  OER.  For  example, Rehman  and  coworkers  prepared  CDs  and  the  mixed  linkers (BTC and bpy) Ni-MOF supported on nickel foam (NF) for OER [99]. The obtained electrocatalyst exhibited improved activity and excellent  stability,  with  an  onset  potential  of $1.5\mathrm{~V~}$ and  an overpotential  of $320\mathrm{\mV}@10\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ .  The  high  OER  catalytic activity could be attributed to the synergistic effect of $\mathrm{CDs@MOF}$ The encapsulated CDs not only increased the active surface area, but also lowered the resistance and enhanced the conductivity of the  composite.  In  addition,  when  hybridized  with  CDs,  the oxidation peaks of $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ to $\mathrm{Ni^{3+}}$ species became stronger and more negative, indicating that there are more highly active nickel species in  this  composite,  thus  further  improving  the  OER  activity. Similarly,  Mou  et  al.  prepared  a  kind  of  porous  composite  of $\mathrm{CQDs_{10}@N i F e–M O F{-A}}$ for  the  OER  [101].  The  composite  is covered  with  an  ultrathin  metal  oxide  layer  (about $1\ \mathrm{nm}\cdot$ )  and there  is  partial  loss  of  lattice  after  calcining  treatment.  In  this composite,  the  grafting  of  CQDs  greatly  enhanced  the conductivity of MOFs and exposed abundant surface-active sites. Meanwhile, the electronic structure of active sites was effectively modulated via the synergy effect between CQDs and NiFe ions as the introduction of CQDs results in the negative shift of the metal redox peak. In addition, the electronic structure of $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{Fe}$ ions was also  regulated  by  their  strong  electronic  interaction  due  to  the lattice disorder from the low-temperature calcination, which also generated  more  active  sites.  As  a  result,  the  catalyst  showed remarkable activity and stability for the OER with an overpotential of $289~\mathrm{mV}$ to achieve $10\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , negligible activity decline for 5000  cycles  test,  and  high  mass  activity  of  91.6 $\mathbf{A\cdotg}^{-1}$ at overpotential of $300\mathrm{mV}$ .
To  expose  more  active  metal  sites,  ultrathin  MOFs  NSs  have been synthesized and studied extensively. For instance, Song et al. successfully  synthesized  a  highly  active  electrocatalyst  of  CQDsCOOH  induced  2D  NiFe-MOF  NSs  for  the  OER  [103].  The formed  NiFe-MOF  NSs  own  uniform,  ultrathin  (only $5\ \mathrm{nm}$ in thickness) and mesoporous structure. The unique structure with exposed rich active sites endowed them with remarkable oxygen and  electrolyte  transfer  capacity.  In  addition,  the  authors employed the Hirschfeld charge to confirm that Fe is the active site for OER, which has a more positive charge than Ni and C sites and thus facilitates the OER process (Fig. 10(a)). In this composite, the functional groups on CQDs surface have great influence on the  electronic  structure  of  the  active  site.  Different  from  the electron-donating  effect  of –OH,  the  electron-withdrawing property  of –COOH  made  the  charge  of  Fe  sites  to  be  more positive.  Therefore,  the  NiFe-MOF $\mathrm{NSs@CQDs–COOH}$ showed outstanding  OER  catalytic  property  with  a  low  overpotential  of $261\ \mathrm{\mV}@10\ \mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2};$ ,  a  small  Tafel  slope  of $56\mathrm{\mV\cdotdec^{-1}}$ and superior stability (Fig. 10(c)). The DFT calculations also indicated that the introduction of CQDs-COOH can significantly reduce the free energy of the rate-determining step to improve the catalytic performance  for  the  OER  (Fig. 10(b)).  However,  excessive introduction of CQDs would block the pores and active sites of MOFs, even resulting in a decline in OER performance.
Compared with 0D carbon, 1D and 2D carbons are proved to be more available to attain superior OER electrocatalysts due to their  high  mechanical  robustness,  structural  flexibility,  good electrical  conductivity  and  large  surface  area.  More  importantly, these  carbons  are  greatly  advantageous  to  drive  MOF  into favorable  morphologies,  such  as  2D  MOF  NS  arrays  with  large specific surfaces. For example, Sreekanth et al. integrated marigold flower-like Ni-MOFs on the surface of MWCNTs and investigated the  OER  catalytic  activity  (Fig. 10(d))  [158].  The  obtained  NiMOFs@CNT  composite  exhibited  improved  activity  with overpotential  of $370~\mathrm{mV}$ to  reach $10\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ,  which  could  be ascribed to its hierarchical micro-flower morphology (Fig. 10(e)). In  this  composite,  the  high  conductivity  of  MWCNTs  not  only promoted the electronic conductivity of the as-obtained composite but  also  accelerated  ion  transport  by  the  linked  network-like structure during redox reactions. Meanwhile, the high surface area of the flower-like MOF provided more active sites for the OER. Thus, the increased number of active sites and ion transport rate enhanced the OER performance of the formed composite. Yu et al. fabricated ultrathin CoNi MOF NSs with MWCNTs to serve as a stable and active OER electrocatalyst in alkaline media [110]. In this  composite,  the  MWCNTs  played  trifunctional  roles.  Firstly, MWCNTs can direct the formation of favorable NSs shape, which could  increase  the  permeability  of  electrolyte  and  facilitate  the adsorption  of $\mathrm{OH-}$ at  the  active  sites.  Secondly,  MWCNTs  can induce abundant defects and the structure re-arrangement of the composite,  leading  to $\pi$ bonding  ultimately,  and  increasing  the capacity of electron transfer and the number of active sites on the electrocatalyst  surface.  Finally,  MWCNTs  contact  strongly  with MOF to regulate the electronic structure of active metals in MOF surface,  which  may  promote  the  adsorption/desorption  of intermediates.  As  a  result,  due  to  the  specific  structure  and synergistic  effect  between  CoNi  MOF  and  MWCNTs,  the  assynthesized hybrid only needs an overpotential of $306\mathrm{V}$ to reach $10\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ,  much  lower  than  that  obtained  from  the  single
component.
Though  the  OER  performance  of  MOF  is  significantly improved via its  specific  and  tuned  NSs  shape  and  hybridizing with  carbon-based  materials,  the  separated  orbitals  due  to  the unbonded  interface  result  in  insufficient  electron  cloud  overlap between the carbon and MOF, thus hindering the faster electron transfer  between  them.  To  boost  the  overlap  of  electron  clouds and  further  increase  the  OER  electrocatalytic  activity,  Hai  et  al. anchored  ultrathin  Ni-MOFs  NSs  on  graphene via the  covalent bond with PBA molecules as covalent linkers [138]. The authors employed a high-throughput periodic DFT workflow to seek the best  anchor  primers  from  1584  kinds  of  candidates,  and  found that  the  unoccupied  orbits  of  PBA  could  offer  high  orbital delocalization and thus support facile routes for electrons transfer. Besides, the covalently linked PBA could induce the ultrathin 2D NSs formation of MOF. Only shuttle-shaped MOF particles can be  obtained  without  PBA.  The  ultrathin  NS  shape  endows  the composite  faster  mass  transport  and  abundant  active  sites exposure.  Hence,  the  attained  Ni-HMOF@GE-PBA  with  high conductivity, fast electron and mass transfer, and rich active sites displayed remarkable OER catalytic performance with much low overpotential of $143\mathrm{mV}$ to reach $50\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ .
Since  the  metal  nodes  of  MOF  are  easy  to  be  oxidized  into hydroxides in the process of OER, recently, researchers introduced hydroxides  into  MOF-carbon  structures  to  improve  the electrocatalytic performance. For example, Wang et al. constructed core-shell conductive MOF/LDH arrays on CC (cMOF/LDH/CC) as  OER  catalyst  [159].  The  cMOF/LDH/CC  composite presented a highly ordered hierarchical porous structure and multi-metallic  nanotube  arrays,  which  are  conducive  to  fast mass  transfer  and  exposure  of  more  metal  active  sites  to electrolyte. In this composite, the multi-metallic metal centers in  the  internal  LDH  cores  were  confirmed  to  be  the  active species.  The  valence  states  of  metals  could  be  partially oxidized  to  the  higher  during  the  OER  process  and  thus promoted  the  adsorption  of  ‒OH  and  electron  conduction from ‒OH to active metal sites. The authors employed a 2D graphene-like  conducting  MOF  with  hexatopic  triphenylene ligands  as  shells  to  effectively  improve  the  electrical conductivity (Fig. 10(h)). Meanwhile, more surface $C^{-}\mathrm{O}$ bonds were  formed  by  introducing  the  organic  linkers,  which facilitated the electrons migration from oxygen to metal active sites. Therefore, because of the rich active sites from CoNiFeLDH  core,  the  high  conductivity  of  cMOF  shell,  and  the synergistic effects at heterojunctions, the cMOF/LDH hybrid exhibited  superior  intrinsic  activity  and  mass  transport  for OER in alkaline solution (Fig. 10(i)). Our group also fabricated 2D  FeNi-LDH  NSs  with  1D  sword-like  FeNi-MOFs  on  CC surface for OER application [125]. The hybrid exhibited improved OER  electrocatalytic  activity  with  a  small  Tafel  slope  of  34.1 $\mathrm{{m}V\cdot d e c^{-1}}$ , a low overpotential of $272{\mathrm{~mV}}@100{\mathrm{~mA}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{cm}}^{-2}$ and high stability,  which  could  be  due  to  the  unique  combined  2D/1D structure. The structure presented as following advantages. First, the  long  MOF $(\sim1.3~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ provides  large  surface  area  to  contact with the electrolyte. Second, the ultrathin 2D LDH NSs $(\sim1.3\:\mathrm{nm})$ and unsaturated porous MOF structure provide abundant active sites.  Third,  the  CC  support  accelerated  charge  migration  and stabilized  the  composite.  Fourth,  the  regular  arrays  facilitate electrolyte  penetration  and  mass  transfer.  Hence,  with  proper MOF/LDH  ratio,  the  formed  hybrid  provided  abundant  active sites,  high  active  area,  fast  charge  transfer  and  mass  transport, resulting in high OER activity.
## 3.3    ORR electrocatalysis
Zeng et al. prepared an ORR electrocatalyst by growing UiO-66- $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ on the surface of cobalt phthalocyanine-modified CNT (UiO$66{\cdot}\mathrm{NO}_{2}@\mathrm{CoCNT})$ [120].  In  this  composite,  the  catalytic  active sites  are  cobalt  phthalocyanines  and  CNT  was  used  as  the conductive backbone. On the other hand, MOF serves as oxygen adsorption pump, whose oxygen supply ability is 17.7-fold higher than CoCNT. As a result, the synergistic effects between the three components enhanced the absorption, migration and reduction of oxygen molecules, and electron transfer, resulting in the excellent ORR performance with $E_{1/2}=0.865\:\mathrm{V}$ and $j_{\mathrm{L}}=5.9\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ .
Kim et al. fabricated a $\mathrm{MOF@mC}$ composite by filling Cu- and Ni-HKUST-1  into  the  pores  of  3D  ordered  mesoporous  carbon for  ORR  [153].  In  this  composite,  the  3D  carbon  plays  an important role, which can not only stabilize and limit the small size  of  MOFs,  but  also  accelerate  the  electron  transport  from MOFs to electrode. Especially, the $\mathrm{{\mC}}$ $24\mathrm{nm}$ in pore size) could effectively shorten the electron pathway within the insulated MOF. The  small  sized  MOFs  exposed  abundant  active  sites  and facilitated  the  contact  between  active  sites  and  electrolyte.  In addition, the connected pores of 3D carbon framework expedited mass  diffusion  through  the  windows.  As  a  result,  the  assynthesized $\mathrm{MOF@mC}$ composite  with  smaller  microporous carbons $\mathrm{(mC)}$ displayed  outstanding  ORR  activity  with remarkable long-term stability and methanol tolerance.
## 3.4    Bifunctional electrocatalysis
Generally,  energy  conversion  devices  contain  not  only  one chemical reaction. Obviously, the water electrolysis consists of the important  anodic  OER  and  cathodic  HER.  Hence,  exploring efficient bifunctional electrocatalysts for both the OER and HER is practically attractive and also becomes a hotspot nowadays.
For  instance,  Zha  et  al.  developed  an  overall  water  splitting electrocatalyst of CoFe-MOF-74/Co/CC hybrid [126]. Experiments  indicated  that  the  as-prepared  CoFe-MOF$74/\mathrm{Co/CC}$ composite  electrode  exhibited  excellent  bifunctional electrocatalytic activities with the overpotentials of only 94 and 226 $\mathrm{mV}$ to  attain $-10\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for  HER  and $20\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for  OER, respectively (Figs. 11(c) and $\boldsymbol{1}\boldsymbol{1}(\mathrm{d}))$ . The outstanding bifunctional catalytic  properties  could  be  ascribed  to  the  synergistic  effect between  the  metal  nodes  of  Fe  and $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{Co},}\end{array}$ the  super-hydrophilic surface  and  excellent  electrical  conductivity  of  the  hybrid electrode.  After  Fe  doping,  the  binding  energy  of  Co $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ decreased  by $0.35~\mathrm{eV}_{:}$ ,  implying  that  an  electron-rich  Co  center was  obtained,  which  is  beneficial  for  the  formation  of  the  key intermediate  (·OOH)  and  thus  enhance  the  OER  property. Meanwhile,  the  three-level  hierarchical  structure  of  CoFe-MOF$74/\mathrm{Co/CC}$ hybrid  played  a  vital  role  in  greatly  improving  the electron conduction and mass transfer. The remained metallic Co enhanced  the  conductivity,  the  MOF-74 in  situ grown  on  Co promoted  the  mechanical  adhesion  and  reduced  the  resistance between  the  composite  interfaces,  and  the  well-ordered nanoarrays exposed more active sites.
Besides, the rechargeable metal-air batteries also involve both of the  discharge  cathodic  ORR  and  the  charge  anodic  OER.  To promote the overall efficiency of this energy system, it is crucial and  urgent  to  develop  outstanding  bifunctional  electrocatalysts with superior activity and stability for both oxygen electrocatalytic reactions [133].
Zheng  et  al.  prepared  bimetallic  CoNi-MOF/rGO  hybrid  for electrocatalysis of both OER and ORR ((Figs. 11(e) and 11(f)). The novel CoNi-MOF/rGO composite consists of uniformly dispersed NSs  on  rGO,  which  exposed  abundant  electrochemically  active sites  and  provided  large  surface  area  to  contact  with  electrolyte. The functional groups on rGO surface make MOF to be tightly combined  with  rGO,  which  significantly  improved  the  electron conductivity.  Moreover,  the  large  surface  contact  area  between MOF  NSs  and  rGO  promoted  the  electron  conduction  and synergistic  effect.  Therefore,  the  CoNi-MOF/rGO  sample exhibited  outstanding  electrocatalytic  activities  due  to  its optimized  electron  conductivity,  rich  active  sites  and  synergistic performance,  and  facilitated  mass  transfer,  chemisorption  and desorption [136].
Xiong  et  al.  also  fabricated  a  yolk-shell  structure  (ZIF$67(\underline{{\alpha}}\mathrm{HMCS})$ with  ZIF-67  as  the  core  and  HMCS  (hollow mesoporous carbon spheres) as the shell for catalyzing the OER and ORR [70]. ZIF-67 with huge size is known to be insulative and low active for electrocatalytic oxygen reactions. However, with confining  small  ZIF-67  particles  into  HMCS,  remarkable  ORR catalytic properties with $E_{1/2}=0.823\mathrm{~V~}$ and $J_{\mathrm{K}}=13.8\ \mathrm{mA{\cdot}c m^{-2}}\ (J_{\mathrm{k}};$ kinetic current density) were displayed. In addition, for OER on the Z $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{IF-}67@\mathrm{HMCS}}.$ current density of $10\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ was reached when an overpotential of only $407\mathrm{mV}$ was needed. The excellent activities were ascribed to the following characteristics. Firstly, the highly  conductive  HMCS  promoted  the  electron  migration. Secondly, the hierarchical pores and small sized ZIF-67 particles confined by the HMCS shortened the diffusion paths to promote the mass transport and exposed more active sites. Thirdly, the ZIF67  provides  rich  coordination  unsaturated  sites $(\mathrm{Co-N}_{x}$ x≤4). Fourthly, the synergistic effect between N-doped HMCS and ZIF67 improved the electrocatalytic activity and the confinement of HMCS enhanced the stability of ZIF-67. Therefore, when applied for  rechargeable  Zn–air  battery,  the  as-obtained  hybrid  catalyst also showed high-power density and excellent long-term stability.
## 4    Summary and outlook
Efficient electrocatalysts are critical for promoting the development of energy conversion technologies. In this review, we highlight  the  latest  development  of  MOF/carbon  composites  on the synthesis strategies and their application in energy conversion, such  as  HER,  OER  and  ORR.  For  preparing  the  MOF/carbon composites,  various  dimensions  of  carbon-based  materials  were supplied,  like  0D  CQDs,  1D  CNTs,  2D  graphene,  3D  porous carbons  and  carbon  frameworks.  The  hybrid  carbon-based materials  can  not  only  improve  the  conductivity  of  the  asprepared composites, but also enhance their stability and activity for  electrocatalysis.  However,  the  catalytic  performances  of MOF/carbon  composites  still  don’t  satisfy  the  practical applications,  and  it  is  needed  to  further  lower  the  overpotential and  increase  the  stability.  For  further  enhancing  the  catalytic properties of the MOF/carbon composites for application in HER, OER  and  ORR  electrocatalysis,  some  personal  perspectives  are proposed, from the design of MOFs, the selection of carbon-based materials,  the  synthesis  strategies  and  the  requirements  for MOF/carbon composites as electrocatalysts.
(1) Design of novel MOFs. The efficient performance of MOFs for  electrocatalysis  can  be  ascribed  to  the  active  components  of organic linkers and metal nodes, which are usually affected by the crystal and pore structures, encapsulated guest molecules and so on.  Hence,  deep  understanding  of  MOFs  is  required  and indispensable for advanced innovations on MOF-based electrocatalysts.  Besides,  for  increasing  the  mass  transport  and electron  transfer,  ultrathin  nanosheets  and  small  nanoparticles having  large  surface  areas  are  also  competitive  morphology  for MOFs, which can also expose more active sites and thus promote electrocatalytic  activities.  Moreover,  for  large-scale  industrial production,  low-cost  and  facile  fabrication  of  MOFs  are  equally important.
(2)  Selection  of  carbon-based  materials.  In  the  MOF/carbon composites, a vital role of the carbon materials is enhancing the conductivity  and  stability  of  MOFs.  Therefore,  a  strong  binding strength  between  MOFs  and  carbons  may  be  favorable  for  the application of these composites in electrocatalysis. In addition, the hybridized  carbons  can  serve  as  regulators  and  even  active ingredients  sometimes.  Therefore,  reasonably  controlling  the structure and morphology of carbon-based materials is significant to promote the activity of these composites.
(3) Synthesis strategies. Due to the formation of carbon-based materials is often accompanied by high temperature, MOF/carbon composites  are  mainly  synthesized  by in  situ and ex  situ technologies.  Compared  with ex  situ technologies,  the in  situ synthesized  composites  commonly  have  stronger  interaction between  MOFs  and  carbons,  which  is  conducive  for  electron migration  in  electrocatalysis.  Besides,  the  morphology  of  the  asobtained  composites  is  strongly  dependent  on  the  fabrication strategies. Therefore, it is worth investigating and developing new synthesis strategies and processes to realize controllable design of MOF/carbon  composites,  which  could  greatly  improve  the electron  transfer  and  catalytic  performance  for  electrocatalytic reactions.
(4) Requirements for MOF/carbon composites as electrocatalysts.  To  design  efficient  electrocatalysts,  we  should firstly  identify  the  main  factors  that  may  suppress  or  limit  the catalytic  performance,  such  as  the  electrical  conductivity,  the loading amount of the catalysts, the stability, the intrinsic activity of  the  active  sites,  mass  transport,  et  al.  In  addition,  during  the electrocatalytic  process,  the  electrocatalysts  may  be  corroded, especially  in  acidic  media  or  at  high  oxidation  potentials.  The corrosion will lead to the dissolution or oxidation of catalysts to high valence state, thus resulting in low long-term stability. Hence, it is promising to strengthen the interaction between MOFs and carbon-based materials.
In summary, it is a hot and significant topic to develop novel nanostructured  electrocatalysts  for  the  important  reactions  of HER,  OER  and  ORR  in  energy  conversion.  Although  certain problems with MOF/carbon composites for HER, OER and ORR remain, their unique structures and remarkable properties render them a class of promising electrocatalysts. Through rational design and  synthesis,  it  is  believed  that  more  MOF/carbon  composites can  be  developed  in  the  further  for  HER,  OER  and  ORR electrocatalysis.
